To benchmark on Centaurus
1. Pull Git repository
2. run "make" in the "par-graphcrawler" directory to compile the code
3. run the batch script to execute the code through slurm. Use the following rules:

    Use the following arguments to run on command line:
    sbatch batch_script.sh <input> <dt> <nbstep> <printevery> <nbthreads> 
    input can be:"<<"\n"
    a number (random initialization)"<<"\n"
    planet (initialize with solar system)"<<"\n"
    a filename (load from file in singleline tsv)"<<"\n";

4. The execution time will be listed at the bottom of the .out file that is generated after execution


Solar System dt = 200, nbstep = 5000000
1 Threads, Time to run: 45.2438s
2 Threads, Time to run: 101.799s
4 Threads, Time to run: 127.451s
8 Threads, Time to run: 196.599s

100 Particles dt = 1, nbstep = 10000
1 Threads, Time to run: 1.6476s
2 Threads, Time to run: 2.61277s
4 Threads, Time to run: 6.20452s
8 Threads, Time to run: 8.1132s


1000 Particles dt = 1, nbstep = 10000
2 Threads, Time to run: 105.742s
4 Threads, Time to run: 136.009s
8 Threads, Time to run: 152.599s

Clearly, I have an issue with my implementation of the assignment. My performance gets consistently worse as the number of threads increases,
which is not what is supposed to happen. I have tried adjusting the level of granularity and reworking my parallel code, but I was never able
to get a meaningful speedup. My main theory is that this is due to context switching overhead, but I would still expect to see some improvement
different granularity levels. Feedback on what I'm doing wrong here would be much appreciated.